in a previous interview you were a bit  critical about how heavily the civil  rights movement relied on the leadership  of Martin Luther King I was just  wondering what you think successful  leadership is whether it's supplemental  to these movements and how leaders can  contribute their time talent and  treasure to these movements without  being the sole propel our critique of  dr. King and remember we love dr. King  and we admired him a great deal we  thought he was bravery personified were  somebody worth following the our  critique of him was that he often  without meaning to said to local people  I'm going to take care of your problems  you don't have to worry about these  things I'll do it and I say this is not  something he was said this was something  they imputed to him and as a consequence  felt themselves less likely or less able  to join in helping themselves so  leadership is always a necessary  component of social change but that  leadership doesn't have to stifle and we  believe that dr. King's persona again  not through his own making but through  the eyes of others stifled their own  ability to act as leaders so with I  remember I was in a hotel in Atlanta  once having a meeting with dr. King and  passed by a bunch of maids in the  hallway and one of them said dr. King  just went by I know all our problems are  going to be solved now apparently they  had some dispute with the hotel but dr.  Cain was not there for their dispute  with the hotel although i'm sure he  sympathized with it he was there for  entirely different purposes but they had  transferred their responsibility to do  something about their condition to this  man who was just passing by so that's  our argument with him and again it was  not a personal argument with the persona  it was the argument with the people who  thought this of him and imputed to him  the ability to solve their problems
 

